  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 315 PM EDT Saturday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Unsettled and humid through Sunday as a frontal \nboundary lifts north later today, followed by a cold frontal passage \nlate Sunday. Numerous showers, thunderstorms, and localized heavy \nrain today-tonight, with scattered showers/storms Sunday.\n\nScattered showers and isolated thunderstorms have started to develop \nacross the area, with the most robust convection developing closer \nto the coast in NE NC thanks to the influence of the sea breeze. \nThough the storms are moving slow, they have been moving just enough \nto keep from causing any hydro concerns just yet, however, it is \nstill early. The main focal point is a formerly stalled boundary \nstarting to lift back northward as a warm front. Current SPC \nmesoanalysis is highlighting PW values of over 2\" across SE VA and \nNE NC where the best showers and thunderstorms currently are \nlocated. Satellite imagery does show some clearing across the \nsouthern half of the forecast area, so temperatures have been able \nto climb into the mid-upper 80s. Across the northern half of the \narea, temperatures have been generally capped in the lower 80s due \nto the cloud cover, though clouds are starting to clear some, so \nthese areas could see temperatures climb a few degrees if this trend \ncontinues. \n\nWhile there is decent instability across the region, the best height \nfalls will be later tonight as a shortwave slides through the area. \nWith the instability and better support aloft not occuring at the \nsame time, the severe threat remains low today, though a few gusty \nshowers cannot be ruled out. With high precipitable water values, \nmodest instability (skinny CAPE profile), and weak steering flow, \nthe environment is favorable for efficient warm rainfall processes \nand localized hydro concerns (supported by an area-wide WPC ERO \nMarginal Risk and a localized upgrade to Slight for portions of the \nI-95 corridor, including metro RIC. Though there is good confidence \nin hydro concerns somewhere across the area, the main question is \nwhere. This hinges on the movement of the front. Though the event is \nalready starting, confidence is too low in any specific location to \nput out a Flood Watch for this afternoon/tonight. Heading into \ntonight, though the best instability does wane overnight, the \naforementioned shortwave will help provide an additional cold air \npool aloft that should be sufficient to keep high chc to likely PoPs \nthrough tomorrow morning. The boundary washes out Sunday, leading to \nnoticeably lower convective coverage for the NW 1/2 of the area, but \nhigh chc to likely PoPs remain in the forecast for the SE and some \nlate day storms are still possible in the NW as well. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Mainly dry with seasonable temperatures and \nsignificantly lower humidity Monday-Wednesday. Temperatures warm \nback above normal late in the week an upper ridge builds back into \nthe region.\n\nThe upper level flow becomes NW on Monday as a strong upper level \ntrough dives SE from the Great Lakes and pushes off the NE and mid-\nAtlantic coast Monday night, with an upper level ridge slowly \nexpanding NE from the lower MS Valley. This looks strong enough to \ndrive the sfc cold front well south of the CWA Monday, and bring \nsignificantly lower dew points that are more typical of September. \nThe models have trended a little farther south and slower with the \nshortwave on Monday, so do expect to see a 3-6 hr window where \nclouds increase and even a few isolated showers develop on Monday \nbefore the much drier air surges in from the NNW during the \nafternoon (with slight chance PoPs lingering through the\nafternoon across the southern areas). \n\nAs high pressure builds in behind the front Monday night, a pleasant \nnight is expected Monday night/Tue AM with lows mostly in the upper \n50s to lower 60s (low-mid 50s are likely in many locations to the NW \nof Richmond). The models have trended a bit slower with the return \nflow of moisture so that now dew points look to remain comfortable \nthrough at least Wednesday as strong sfc high pressure (nearly 1030 \nmb), sets up from off the New England coast SW into the Carolina \ncoastal plain through midweek. Highs will stay mostly in the low-mid \n80s through Wed with lows in the 50s to lower 60s (locally warmer at \nthe immediate coast). The upper level ridge is forecast to expand E-\nNE to the TN Valley and central Appalachians Thu-Fri. Temperatures \nclimb back to above normal for mid- September, with highs generally \nin the mid-upper 80s. Low end diurnal rain chances return Thu-\nFri.",
